#---
# purpose: "the Map surface over a flat sorted array: size, keys, values, to_list, has_key?, delete"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# # map_surface
#
# A map here is a FLAT SORTED ARRAY, not a HAMT, so this module's emphasis is
# the opposite of Elixir's: the bulk functions are the surface to reach for, and
# `put/3` and `delete/2` each copy the whole array.
#
# `delete/2` did not exist at all before this -- there was no `fz_map_delete`
# anywhere in the runtime.
#
# ## Why binary keys
#
# Everything printed here is keyed by BINARIES, deliberately. Elixir orders
# binary keys by content and fz now does too (fz-5xp.48), so iteration order
# matches. ATOM-keyed order cannot match: Elixir orders atoms by an internal
# identity fz does not share (fz-5xp.50), and fz also renders `%{:a => 1}`
# where Elixir renders `%{a: 1}` (fz-5xp.51). Either would sink an oracle.
#
# The goal program is on the matching side of that line: JSON object keys are
# strings.
def main() do
  m = %{"b" => 2, "a" => 1, "c" => 3}

  dbg(Map.size(m))
  dbg(Map.keys(m))
  dbg(Map.values(m))
  dbg(Map.to_list(m))

  dbg(Map.has_key?(m, "a"))
  dbg(Map.has_key?(m, "zz"))

  dbg(Map.to_list(Map.delete(m, "b")))
  dbg(Map.to_list(Map.delete(m, "zz")))
  dbg(Map.size(Map.delete(m, "a")))

  # a key built at runtime is the same key
  built = "a"
  dbg(Map.has_key?(m, built))
  dbg(Map.to_list(Map.delete(m, built)))
end
